const origenSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('evidence', 'A large achievement in a damaged archive', [
    'Origen of Alexandria was among the most productive Christian scholars of the third century, yet his surviving corpus is not a transparent shelf of authorial originals. Some important works remain substantially in Greek, especially Against Celsus and parts of biblical commentaries. Many homilies and commentaries survive wholly or partly in Latin translations; On First Principles is complete only through Rufinus’s Latin, beside Greek excerpts selected for other purposes. Still other teachings are reported by admirers, opponents, conciliar dossiers, and catena compilers. A responsible portrait must mark these channels before asking what Origen taught, because a hostile excerpt and a continuous Greek argument do not carry equal weight.',
    'This transmission problem is unusually entangled with controversy. Rufinus said that he corrected passages he believed heretics had corrupted, while Jerome offered rival translations or excerpts and accused him of concealing error. Later anti-Origenist writers assembled propositions for condemnation, sometimes drawing on developments among Origen’s readers rather than securely preserved sentences by Origen himself. Modern critical work can compare witnesses but cannot recreate every lost Greek page. The proper method is therefore graded: give priority to continuous texts and converging evidence, identify translation or fragment mediation, and refuse to turn later “Origenism” into a simple index of Origen’s own settled doctrines.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'plotinus'], ['On First Principles', 'Against Celsus']),
  section('life', 'Alexandria, Caesarea, and the making of a Christian scholar', [
    'Origen was born around 185 in Alexandria, an intellectual and commercial center where Jewish, Christian, and Greek traditions met. Eusebius says that his father Leontius died in the persecution of 202 and depicts the young Origen as eager for martyrdom. The broad setting and his early teaching career are plausible, but picturesque details—such as his mother hiding his clothes—belong to Eusebius’s exemplary narrative. Origen acquired formidable literary and philosophical training, taught Christian students, and worked with patrons, stenographers, and copyists. The older image of a solitary genius understates the social and material organization required for his enormous exegetical output.',
    'Conflict with Bishop Demetrius accompanied Origen’s move from Alexandria to Caesarea, probably in the early 230s. Eusebius connects the rupture with ordination outside Alexandria and earlier disputes, but the exact institutional and personal causes remain debated. Caesarea became the setting for preaching, advanced teaching, and many later works. During the persecution under Decius, Origen was imprisoned and tortured; he died a few years later, conventionally around 253 or 254. These events matter philosophically because his scholarship served changing ecclesial settings: classroom instruction, public preaching, scriptural textual work, pastoral counsel, and defense of Christianity each generated a different form of writing.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['Ecclesiastical History VI']),
  section('scripture', 'Scripture as a many-leveled field of education', [
    'Origen’s central intellectual practice was interpretation of scripture. He compared textual witnesses, studied word usage, compiled the Hexapla’s parallel columns, wrote commentaries and scholia, and preached homilies. His famous account in On First Principles distinguishes bodily, psychic, and spiritual senses by analogy with the human person, but this is not a mechanical code assigning three meanings to every verse. Literal wording, moral formation, doctrinal coherence, and the drama of salvation interact. Difficulties, impossibilities, or apparent divine unworthiness in the text can function as prompts that prevent a reader from stopping at an inadequate surface understanding.',
    'Calling this method “allegory” is useful only if it does not imply arbitrary symbolism. Origen argues within a rule of faith, attends closely to language and cross-reference, and expects interpretations to cohere with the character of God revealed in Christ. At the same time, modern readers need not assume that every spiritual reading follows from historical-linguistic evidence as now understood. His aim was formative: the Logos who addresses the church in scripture leads readers from elementary perception toward moral and intellectual transformation. Exegesis is thus epistemology and spiritual practice together—the purification of attention through which one learns to recognize a single divine pedagogy across diverse texts.',
    'The Hexapla shows the material discipline beneath that aspiration. Origen arranged Hebrew text and several Greek versions in parallel, marking agreements and differences rather than pretending the received Greek wording had no history. The enormous work is lost except for fragments and later traces, so its exact form and use must be reconstructed cautiously. Even so, it demonstrates that spiritual reading and textual criticism were not rivals for him. Establishing words, comparing translations, and noticing variants could serve the larger theological task. His interpretations may exceed modern philology, but they did not begin from indifference to textual form.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['On First Principles IV', 'Hexapla']),
  section('god-logos', 'Father, Logos, Spirit, and the danger of later vocabularies', [
    'Origen develops Christian teaching about Father, Son, and Spirit before the Council of Nicaea fixed later conciliar formulas. He describes the Father as the ultimate source, the Son as eternally generated Wisdom and Logos, and the Spirit as a distinct divine hypostasis active in sanctification. He rejects the idea that God first existed without Wisdom and then produced it for creation; generation must not be imagined as a temporal event or bodily division. The Logos contains rational principles of created things and makes the transcendent Father intelligible according to creatures’ capacities. This is philosophical theology constructed from scripture rather than a detachable import from Platonism.',
    'His language nevertheless includes asymmetries later readers could call subordinationist, and surviving witnesses complicate exact judgment. Terms such as ousia, hypostasis, and homoousios did not yet bear their later Nicene technical stability. Rufinus may smooth formulations; hostile excerpts may sharpen them. It is anachronistic either to certify Origen as simply Nicene in advance or to judge every pre-Nicene distinction by a fourth-century vocabulary. The secure point is that he argued for eternal, real distinctions within divine life while ordering the Son and Spirit to the Father as source. How individual formulations relate to later orthodoxy requires textual and historical qualification, not one retrospective label.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'metaphysics'], ['plato'], ['On First Principles I', 'Commentary on John']),
  section('creation-freedom', 'Creation, rational creatures, and moral difference', [
    'For Origen, the good creator does not make rational beings wicked, privileged, or damned by nature. Their diverse conditions must be understood through created freedom and divine pedagogy rather than arbitrary divine favoritism. On First Principles explores prior causes for differences among rational creatures, cosmic orders, and embodiments. Yet the popular story that originally disembodied minds grew bored, cooled in love, and fell into bodies relies heavily on contested or hostile witnesses. Secure texts affirm created rational agency, different grades of embodiment, and repeated divine correction; they do not warrant narrating one fully documented prehistoric fall as an uncontested Origenian myth.',
    'Embodiment itself should not be equated with evil. Origen rejects bodily transmigration in Against Celsus, interprets resurrection as transformation rather than abandonment of identity, and can speak of bodies appropriate to different conditions of rational life. Matter is created and governed by God; evil has no independent substance but arises through misdirected will. Because freedom remains, rational creatures can change for better or worse. Divine foreknowledge does not cause their choices, as On Prayer argues. This combination lets Origen defend providence without making God the author of moral inequality, though the precise relation between preexistence, embodiment, and the resurrection body remains one of the corpus’s most transmission-sensitive problems.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['plato'], ['On First Principles II–III', 'On Prayer']),
  section('salvation', 'Christ, pedagogy, punishment, and disputed restoration', [
    'Salvation is the Logos’s work of healing rational creatures and restoring their capacity to know and love God. Origen uses multiple scriptural models for Christ’s action—teacher, physician, victor, sacrifice, ransom—without reducing them to a single later theory of atonement. Punishment is frequently remedial: divine fire exposes and consumes vice as a physician’s painful treatment addresses disease. Progress may extend beyond death through differentiated instruction and purification. This account makes justice purposive rather than retaliatory, yet it does not make moral effort dispensable. Freedom, habit, repentance, ecclesial practice, and grace belong to the cure.',
    'Whether Origen taught the guaranteed salvation of every rational creature, including the devil, remains disputed. Texts support a far-reaching hope that God’s enemies may be subdued and that divine pedagogy seeks restoration, but other passages preserve the possibility of persistent refusal or distinguish submission from blessed communion. Later Origenists developed restoration schemes, while opponents attributed propositions through condemnatory summaries. The safest conclusion is neither that Origen clearly taught the later condemned package nor that universal restoration is absent from his thought. He furnished powerful resources for universalist readings, embedded in freedom and purification, while the surviving evidence leaves the scope and certainty of the final outcome contested.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ethics', 'metaphysics'], ['gregory-nyssa'], ['On First Principles I–III', 'Against Celsus']),
  section('celsus', 'Philosophical argument in a religiously plural empire', [
    'Against Celsus answers a learned second-century critic whose True Doctrine survives only through Origen’s quotations and paraphrases. Origen defends Christian monotheism, incarnation, resurrection, scripture, and the social character of the church while contesting Celsus’s claims about novelty, credulity, and civic disloyalty. The work shows extensive engagement with Platonist categories and Greek literature, but engagement is not surrender. Origen tests philosophical claims against scripture and Christian practice, praises truths he finds among Greeks, and attacks polytheistic cult, fatalism, and accounts of providence that exclude ordinary people from divine care.',
    'The debate also displays a philosophy of audience. Christianity need not offer identical instruction to everyone: narratives, commandments, worship, and advanced contemplation can address different capacities while participating in one truth. This graded pedagogy can appear elitist, especially when Origen contrasts simpler believers with perfected knowers. Yet he also argues that the gospel’s accessibility and formation of morally serious communities count as evidence against a culture that reserves philosophy for a small educated class. Against Celsus therefore belongs neither solely to apologetics nor to the history of Platonism. It is a sustained contest over what reason, revelation, practice, and social transformation can each contribute to truth.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'epistemology', 'ancient-greek'], ['plato'], ['Against Celsus']),
  section('practice', 'Prayer, ascetic attention, and spiritual senses', [
    'On Prayer begins from an apparent contradiction: if God foreknows events and providence orders the world, why ask for anything? Origen answers that foreknowledge does not necessitate choice and that prayer belongs within providence as a real cause and relationship. Mature prayer seeks participation in divine goods rather than the manipulation of God. His interpretation of the Lord’s Prayer joins doctrinal reflection, moral examination, bodily posture, communal orientation, and attention to scripture. Prayer thus trains desire: its value is not measured only by whether an external request is granted, because the person praying is being reordered toward the Father through the Logos and Spirit.',
    'The language of spiritual senses extends this formative picture. Scriptural hearing, sight, taste, touch, and smell name capacities by which a purified mind discerns divine realities. They are not simply paranormal duplicates of bodily organs, nor an argument that bodies are worthless. Origen’s asceticism disciplines embodied habits so that sensation and imagination no longer rule judgment. Martyrdom, virginity, study, almsgiving, and prayer can become modes of witness, but their value lies in love and freedom rather than suffering itself. Reports of self-castration remain uncertain and should not organize his ethics. His mature ideal is intelligent participation in God, cultivated through repeated practices of interpretation and desire.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-mind', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['On Prayer', 'Commentary on the Song of Songs']),
  section('condemnations', 'Origen, Origenists, and the politics of a legacy', [
    'Origen’s influence spread through admirers who did not all read him alike. Pamphilus defended him; Rufinus translated and harmonized him; Jerome moved from admiration to attack; Evagrius, Didymus, and later monastic theologians developed themes associated with rational creatures, contemplation, and restoration. Fourth-century controversy and sixth-century imperial and ecclesiastical condemnations produced lists of “Origenist” errors. The precise conciliar status and textual targets of the anathemas associated with Constantinople in 553 are historically complex. It is too simple to say that one council neutrally read Origen’s books and condemned a fixed set of his personal opinions.',
    'Distinguishing Origen from Origenism does not immunize him from criticism. Some speculative proposals were genuinely risky, his hierarchical language about rational maturity can be troubling, and later readers found real materials for disputed doctrines in his corpus. The distinction instead enforces historical responsibility: ask which text survives, in which language, through whose selection, and whether a later system repeats or transforms it. Origen’s afterlife demonstrates how theological identities are constructed through translation, excerpting, institutional conflict, and retrospective orthodoxy. His legacy is therefore not only a list of doctrines but also a lesson in how archives and controversies govern what posterity thinks an author said.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'epistemology'], ['gregory-nyssa'], ['Apology for Origen', 'On First Principles']),
  section('reading', 'A source-conscious route through Origen', [
    'Begin with On Prayer, chapters 3–6 and 12–20. It shows Origen reasoning from scripture about freedom, providence, desire, and practice in a substantially surviving Greek work. Next sample Against Celsus, especially Books I, III, IV, and VII, observing how he reconstructs an opponent while making a public case for Christian philosophy. Only then approach On First Principles. Read Rufinus’s preface and a modern edition’s account of Greek and Latin witnesses before drawing doctrinal conclusions; record when a passage is Rufinus, a Philocalia excerpt, a fragment, or a hostile parallel rather than silently merging them.',
    'A second stage should pair biblical commentary with reception history. The Commentary on John reveals how Logos theology emerges inside close exegesis; selected homilies show what teaching becomes in a congregation. Trigg and Heine provide different modern maps of his career and scholarship, while the specialist reference helps identify disputes that older summaries flatten. Throughout, replace the question “Was Origen orthodox?” with historically sharper questions: which vocabulary was available before Nicaea, what problem was a proposal solving, how securely is its wording transmitted, and how did later Origenists and critics transform it? That discipline makes his speculative daring visible without confusing reconstruction with possession of the lost Greek originals.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'epistemology'], ['gregory-nyssa'], ['On Prayer', 'Against Celsus', 'On First Principles', 'Commentary on John']),
];

