const gregorySections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('context', 'A philosophical bishop in a contested Christian empire', [
    'Gregory of Nyssa wrote in the later fourth century, when imperial support had not ended Christian disagreement but raised the institutional stakes of it. He belonged to the Cappadocian family and circle associated with his brother Basil of Caesarea, his sister Macrina, and Gregory of Nazianzus. Arguments about how Father, Son, and Spirit are one God while irreducibly distinct shaped his technical vocabulary; disputes with Eunomius forced him to ask what human concepts and names can know of God. Scriptural commentary, ascetic writing, preaching, ecclesial diplomacy, and doctrinal controversy therefore belong to one intellectual career rather than to separate “mystical” and “philosophical” Gregories.',
    'His corpus is broad and internally developing. Against Eunomius, On the Making of Man, The Great Catechism, On the Soul and the Resurrection, Life of Moses, homilies on Ecclesiastes and the Song of Songs, and shorter works address different audiences and occasions. Philosophical terms drawn from Platonist, Aristotelian, Stoic, medical, and rhetorical traditions are transformed within biblical arguments. Calling Gregory a Platonist can illuminate intellectual resources, but it cannot explain the linear drama of creation, incarnation, resurrection, and communal worship. Nor should later Byzantine distinctions simply be read back into his vocabulary as though their mature technical form were already fixed.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ancient-greek', 'metaphysics'], ['origen', 'plato'], ['Against Eunomius', 'Life of Moses']),
  section('life', 'Family, episcopacy, loss, and uncertain chronology', [
    'Gregory was born in Cappadocia, conventionally around 335, into a wealthy Christian family. Details of his education and early profession are sparse; his writings show rhetorical training, but claims that one sibling supplied all formal education exceed the evidence. Basil appointed him bishop of the small see of Nyssa in the 370s. Gregory was deposed during pro-Homoean imperial ascendancy, later returned, and after Basil’s death in 379 took a more prominent role in ecclesiastical affairs. He participated in the Council of Constantinople in 381 and undertook further missions, though the exact importance assigned to him by later praise should not be projected backward without qualification.',
    'The deaths of Basil and Macrina in 379 form a decisive literary and emotional context. Life of Macrina and On the Soul and the Resurrection make Macrina an ascetic exemplar and philosophical teacher; their literary artistry does not make her contribution unreal, but neither work is a stenographic deathbed record. Gregory continued writing into the 390s and disappears from secure evidence after about 394 or 395. His career resists a simple narrative of reluctant administrator becoming pure contemplative. Episcopal conflict, grief, doctrinal polemic, ascetic communities, and scriptural interpretation repeatedly reshape the same questions about creaturely change, divine infinity, freedom, and the human journey into likeness with God.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ancient-greek'], ['origen'], ['Life of Macrina', 'On the Soul and the Resurrection']),
  section('knowledge', 'Why an infinite God exceeds every definition', [
    'Gregory’s reply to Eunomius turns on the difference between God’s reality and the names finite knowers use. Eunomius treated “unbegotten” as disclosing the divine essence and supporting a hierarchy in which the Son is unlike the Father. Gregory argues that names arise from human conceptual activity and identify what may truly be said about God without comprehending the divine nature as such. “Unbegotten” marks a relation or manner of existence, not an exhaustive definition. God is not one item within a genus available for classification, and no concept encloses the infinite source of all created being.',
    'This is not simple skepticism or a ban on theology. Scripture gives names, actions disclose divine goodness, and reason can expose contradictions in false accounts. Knowledge is real but proportioned: creatures know that God is and learn from divine activity, while what God is in essence remains beyond conceptual capture. Gregory’s polemical setting matters because incomprehensibility protects both divine transcendence and the equality of the Son: the opponent cannot claim an exhaustive concept of the Father and then exclude the Son from it. Later readers connect this grammar to apophatic theology, but Gregory’s own argument remains Trinitarian, scriptural, and directed against a particular theory of naming.',
    'The controversy also disciplines historical comparison. Gregory uses shared philosophical vocabulary about substance, activity, relation, privation, and infinity, but neither side is doing philosophy in isolation from biblical exegesis and church allegiance. An argument may answer Eunomius’s inference rather than state a freestanding theory of language for every object. Conversely, the theological occasion does not make the reasoning philosophically irrelevant. It asks a general question with lasting force: when a concept identifies something truly, what licenses the stronger claim that it captures the thing’s essence? Gregory’s answer separates successful reference from exhaustive comprehension.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'philosophy-of-language', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['pseudo-dionysius'], ['Against Eunomius']),
  section('creation', 'Creation is good, contingent, and ordered toward transformation', [
    'Gregory distinguishes the uncreated, changeless divine life from created reality, which begins and is intrinsically capable of change. Mutability makes moral failure possible, but it is not itself evil: without change, a creature could not grow in goodness. In On the Making of Man, cosmology culminates in humanity as a living image able to know and govern, while the Great Catechism places creation within a history of fall, incarnation, healing, and resurrection. The world is neither an eternal rival to God nor a prison produced by a lesser maker. Its dependence, order, materiality, and temporal movement belong to the good creator’s project.',
    'Gregory draws on ancient physiology and cosmology that modern science has superseded, and his use of Genesis joins literal sequence, philosophical analysis, and figurative interpretation in ways that do not fit a contemporary science-versus-religion binary. His claim that humanity was considered corporately in the first creation has generated difficult debates about universal nature and individual persons. Zachhuber traces tensions rather than one timeless anthropology. A careful reader should therefore avoid converting Gregory’s “humanity” into either a Platonic Form detached from bodies or a modern population concept. It names a created nature realized in embodied persons and gathered into a providential history.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-religion', 'philosophy-of-science'], ['origen'], ['On the Making of Man', 'The Great Catechism']),
  section('embodiment', 'Image, body, sex, and the whole human creature', [
    'The image of God is not located in one bodily feature, nor is it exhausted by rationality considered in isolation. Gregory associates it with freedom, virtue, relational and royal capacities, and the calling of humanity as a whole. In On the Making of Man he speculates that sexual differentiation answers God’s foreknowledge of the fall and mortality, a difficult proposal that has received sharply different interpretations. It can relativize present gender hierarchy by denying sexual difference a divine archetype, yet it can also depreciate sexed embodiment when read through an angelic ideal. Modern appropriation should preserve both possibilities rather than recruiting Gregory prematurely for one position.',
    'Body and soul nevertheless form one human life. On the Soul and the Resurrection uses philosophical arguments and the figure of Macrina to defend continued identity and bodily resurrection. Matter disperses, but divine knowledge can preserve the relation between soul and the elements that constituted its body; resurrection transforms corruption without replacing the person with someone else. Gregory’s medical analogies and ancient embryology cannot simply be modernized, but his basic challenge remains significant: personal continuity must include embodied history without requiring that every temporary material condition be restored unchanged. Salvation heals the entire creature, so “spiritual” development cannot mean escaping everything bodily.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-mind', 'philosophy-of-religion', 'ethics'], [], ['On the Making of Man', 'On the Soul and the Resurrection']),
  section('freedom-evil', 'Freedom, habit, and evil without substance', [
    'Created freedom images divine self-direction, but because creaturely will is mutable it can turn from the good toward apparent goods. Evil has no independent nature; it is a privation, distortion, or parasitic condition of a good power. This preserves the goodness of creation and assigns moral difference neither to matter nor to a divine act that makes some natures wicked. Yet freedom is not a single isolated choice. Desire forms habits, social arrangements train perception, and vice can enslave the agent who chose it. Gregory’s moral psychology therefore joins responsibility to a therapeutic account of how damaged agency must be liberated.',
    'The relation between divine foreknowledge, providence, and freedom is especially important for eschatology. If God heals all, does choice become unreal? If freedom can refuse without end, can restoration be universal? Gregory often describes punishment as purification and evil as finite because it lacks positive being, while insisting that virtue is freely embraced. Interpretations differ on whether eventual consent is guaranteed, elicited by truth, or expressed as confident hope. It is inadequate either to solve the tension by making salvation coercive or to erase universal-sounding texts. The tension reveals his deeper project: divine goodness must defeat evil without destroying the agency it created as good.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['origen'], ['The Great Catechism', 'On the Soul and the Resurrection']),
  section('progress', 'Perpetual progress toward inexhaustible goodness', [
    'Life of Moses presents virtue through a scriptural itinerary: departure, ascent, darkness, continuing desire, and service. Moses’ encounters with God do not culminate in possession of a bounded object. Because the divine good is infinite, participation can always deepen; satisfaction generates renewed desire rather than boredom or deficiency. Modern scholarship often calls this epektasis, borrowing Paul’s language of stretching forward. The idea joins metaphysics and ethics: an infinite goal makes perfection dynamic, while a mutable creature can use change not merely to fall but to advance without a final limit.',
    'Perpetual progress also changes how darkness is read. In Gregory’s later account, Moses moves from light into cloud and then into divine darkness, learning that the one sought exceeds every concept and image. Unknowing is not ignorance celebrated for itself; it follows disciplined interpretation and moral purification. Nor does ascent end in private ecstasy. Moses descends to guide a people, and Gregory’s ascetic ideal ties contemplation to virtue, community, and care. Later mystical readers rightly found a major source here, but “mysticism” should not detach the work from biblical exegesis or imply that a technique gives immediate mastery of God.',
  ], ['ethics', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['pseudo-dionysius'], ['The Life of Moses']),
  section('eschatology', 'Resurrection, purification, and the universal-restoration dispute', [
    'Gregory describes resurrection as the creator’s restoration of human nature from corruption, grounded in Christ’s assumption of humanity and victory over death. The Great Catechism and On the Soul and the Resurrection often portray judgment through medical or metallurgical images: painful processes remove what is alien so that the good creature can be restored. Several passages anticipate a condition in which evil is eliminated and God is all in all. These claims explain why many specialists read Gregory as affirming universal salvation more confidently than a mere wish. They also belong to distinct genres whose dramatic, catechetical, and exegetical contexts matter.',
    'Debate persists about scope, consistency, and later reception. Some interpreters emphasize explicit universal language; others point to warnings about punishment, freedom, and apparently lasting exclusion, or distinguish restoration of human nature from the destiny of every person and demon. “Apokatastasis” itself can misleadingly import a later condemned Origenist package. A responsible account states that universal-restoration readings have strong textual support while avoiding the claim that every eschatological passage forms one unambiguous system. Gregory’s distinctive difficulty is productive: purification must be serious, freedom real, resurrection embodied, and divine goodness finally victorious, but the exact logical relation among those commitments remains disputed.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ethics', 'metaphysics'], ['origen'], ['The Great Catechism', 'On the Soul and the Resurrection']),
  section('social-ethics', 'Likeness to God in economic and communal life', [
    'Gregory’s ascent language can obscure the social sharpness of his preaching. Homilies condemn the ownership of human beings, attacks on the poor, hoarded wealth, and pride that treats another bearer of the divine image as a commodity. His critique of slavery is remarkable in its ancient setting because it questions the master’s asserted right to price what belongs to God. It should not be converted casually into a modern rights theory or a documented political abolition program, but neither should it be reduced to a metaphor. Anthropology generates ethical claims about bodies, labor, possession, and relations of domination.',
    'Virtue is imitation of divine generosity within creaturely limits. Almsgiving matters, but Gregory asks more radically how wealth is acquired and why common goods become instruments of superiority. Ascetic discipline addresses appetite while ecclesial community provides practices of worship, care, and instruction. These social dimensions also qualify individualistic readings of perpetual progress: likeness to God is tested in how one encounters vulnerable neighbors. His own elite status and episcopal office remain relevant limits, and the corpus does not supply a complete social program. Still, the image doctrine makes contempt for another person a theological and philosophical contradiction rather than merely a failure of private kindness.',
  ], ['ethics', 'political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-religion'], [], ['Homilies on Ecclesiastes', 'On the Love of the Poor']),
  section('reading', 'How to follow Gregory without flattening the corpus', [
    'Begin with Life of Moses, Book II, but keep its scriptural episodes beside the philosophical movement from light to darkness and from attainment to further desire. Then read On the Making of Man, especially chapters 5, 16–18, and 22–30, noting both its defense of embodied humanity and its difficult account of sexual difference. On the Soul and the Resurrection should follow as a dialogue shaped by grief and by Macrina’s authority. The Great Catechism, chapters 5–8, 21–26, and 35–40, supplies a more public synthesis of freedom, incarnation, purification, and resurrection.',
    'Secondary reading should stage disagreements rather than eliminate them. Zachhuber clarifies the philosophical complexity and development of “human nature”; Ludlow’s studies test modern appropriations and the case for universal salvation; the encyclopedia offers a broad orientation that should not decide contested points by itself. Ask of each claim which work, genre, and polemical setting supports it. That practice prevents three common reductions: Gregory as merely a baptized Platonist, as a disembodied mystic of infinite ascent, or as a single-issue witness for or against universalism. His importance lies in holding transcendence, embodiment, freedom, transformation, and eschatological hope in sustained, sometimes unresolved relation.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], ['origen', 'pseudo-dionysius'], ['The Life of Moses', 'On the Making of Man', 'On the Soul and the Resurrection', 'The Great Catechism']),
];

