const dionysiusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('persona', 'An apostolic voice written around the year 500', [
    'The author called Pseudo-Dionysius wrote as Dionysius the Areopagite, the Athenian converted by Paul in Acts 17. The treatises address Timothy, mention apostolic figures, and construct a teacher named Hierotheus, creating the literary world of a first-century witness. Modern scholarship, however, places the corpus in the late fifth or early sixth century. Its dependence on later Platonism, especially Proclus, and its liturgical and doctrinal environment rule out apostolic authorship. The numeric dates used for an atlas marker are therefore placement anchors for an anonymous author, not a birth-and-death chronology. Nothing securely identifies his name, monastery, episcopal office, or city.',
    '“Pseudo-” identifies this authorship problem; it should not become a shortcut for dismissing the writing as a crude fraud. Ancient pseudonymous composition could claim authoritative continuity through a persona, though the corpus undeniably acquired influence because readers accepted apostolic credentials. Early disputes over authenticity and doctrine, the first clear uses in the sixth century, and John of Scythopolis’s scholia belong to the reception history. The safest description is a late-antique Christian author, probably working in the eastern Roman or Syrian cultural sphere, who deliberately writes within an apostolic fiction while transforming a recent philosophical and liturgical inheritance.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'neoplatonism', 'ancient-greek'], ['proclus'], ['Corpus Dionysiacum']),
  section('corpus', 'Four treatises, ten letters, and conspicuous missing books', [
    'The surviving corpus contains Divine Names, Mystical Theology, Celestial Hierarchy, Ecclesiastical Hierarchy, and ten letters. It also refers to works such as Theological Representations and Symbolic Theology that do not survive and may be fictional components of the persona. This matters because modern outlines often treat the extant books as a complete system whose planned order is certain. The works instead cross-reference a larger library, and their own headings or subdivisions may have editorial histories. Readers should identify which treatise performs which task before assembling a single Dionysian metaphysics.',
    'Divine Names studies scriptural predicates such as Good, Being, Life, Wisdom, and One. Mystical Theology enacts an ascent through affirmation and negation toward union beyond ordinary knowing. Celestial Hierarchy interprets scriptural angelic names and symbols; Ecclesiastical Hierarchy reads baptism, Eucharist, consecration, monastic profession, and burial as ordered rites of purification, illumination, and perfection. The letters clarify themes but do not supply a biography. Together these works connect theology, exegesis, liturgy, and ontology. None should be isolated as the “real” mystical philosophy while the hierarchical texts are dismissed as institutional decoration.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-language'], [], ['Divine Names', 'Mystical Theology', 'Celestial Hierarchy', 'Ecclesiastical Hierarchy']),
  section('procession-return', 'Goodness goes forth and gathers creatures back', [
    'Dionysian causality is often summarized as procession and return. Because the divine Good lacks envy, it gives being, life, intelligence, order, and beauty according to each creature’s capacity. Effects display their source without containing or diminishing it; multiplicity proceeds from unity while the source remains beyond participation. Creatures return by turning their powers toward the Good, receiving illumination, and communicating gifts onward. This is not a temporal story in which beings physically travel out of God and later reverse direction. It is a grammar of dependence, manifestation, desire, and ordered participation operating throughout creation.',
    'The pattern bears close comparison with Proclus, whose remaining-procession-return structures and treatment of causality provide important antecedents. Dionysius does not simply copy a pagan diagram and add Christian names. He identifies the source with the scripturally named triune God, centers Jesus, angels, church rites, and biblical symbols, and refuses a populated order of divine henads. Yet “Christianization” should not conceal how deeply late Platonist concepts shape his questions and vocabulary. Influence is secure at the textual and structural level; the author’s schooling, personal contact with Proclus, or exact institutional location remains unknown.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'neoplatonism', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['proclus', 'iamblichus'], ['Divine Names']),
  section('names', 'True divine names that never capture their source', [
    'Divine Names begins with a rule of theological speech: use the scriptural disclosures appropriate to the subject rather than claiming an independent vision of the divine essence. Names such as Good, Being, Life, Wisdom, Power, Justice, and Peace are true because God causes and precontains their perfections in a manner beyond creatures. They are not definitions placing God within a class. The same source can be named from its gifts and remain beyond every name, since a cause is not limited to the mode in which one effect receives it. Theology therefore requires both confident praise and disciplined reserve.',
    'The sequence of names also articulates a causal order. “Good” extends even beyond being, while being, life, and wisdom mark increasingly determinate participations. The exact philosophical relation between these names, divine differentiations, and Trinitarian persons has produced major interpretive disputes. It is safest not to treat the chapters as a simple ladder of substances or to equate each name with a separate entity. Dionysius is describing how one transcendent cause is manifested across different capacities and how scriptural language leads creatures toward that cause. Naming is simultaneously metaphysical analysis, prayerful hymn, and exercise in intellectual humility.',
    'Trinitarian and Christological claims prevent this theology from becoming an impersonal account of an abstract One. The corpus names Father, Son, and Spirit while emphasizing divine unity, and it speaks of Jesus as entering human conditions without surrendering transcendence. Letter 4’s compressed language about Christ’s divine-human activity later required interpretation in Christological controversy. The point is not that the corpus neatly anticipates every later formula. It is that procession, names, hierarchy, and return operate within a specifically Christian economy of incarnation and salvation. Comparisons with Proclus must therefore track both conceptual dependence and doctrinal redirection.',
    'Prayer likewise precedes analysis. Dionysius compares prayer not to pulling a distant divine power down but to drawing oneself along an already present chain of light. This image locates inquiry inside receptive practice: the source is universally present, yet a participant must be turned and prepared to receive according to capacity. Philosophical method is therefore neither autonomous deduction nor passive submission. Scriptural naming, inherited teaching, communal rite, rational distinction, and prayer cooperate. A reader may question the authority structure behind that cooperation, but omitting it produces a misleadingly modern Dionysius who offers only a theory of predication.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'metaphysics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['proclus'], ['Divine Names I–V']),
  section('apophasis', 'Affirmation, negation, and the negation of negation', [
    'Apophatic theology does not mean that negative sentences are simply accurate descriptions while positive ones are false. Dionysius affirms names on the basis of divine causality, negates them because God does not possess their creaturely mode, and finally surpasses the opposition between affirmation and denial. Saying that God is not “being” does not classify God as nonbeing; it denies that the source is bounded by what creatures mean by being. The short Mystical Theology intensifies this movement as Moses leaves sensible and intelligible objects behind and enters the darkness associated with union beyond speech and conceptual mastery.',
    'The endpoint remains disputed. Some readings interpret union as an extraordinary form of knowledge, others as affective experience, liturgical participation, or a transformation beyond the ordinary knowing subject. The text does not license a detailed phenomenology of a private mystical episode. Darkness is not nihilism, irrationalism, or proof that any statement about God is interchangeable with any other. It follows scriptural naming, prayer, purification, and ordered contemplation. Even negations must be relinquished if they become concepts the mind possesses. Dionysian unknowing is thus a critique of conceptual idolatry practiced from within an elaborate theology, not a retreat from argument or communal worship.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'philosophy-of-language', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['gregory-nyssa'], ['Mystical Theology I–V']),
  section('symbols', 'Why unlike images can disclose better than attractive ones', [
    'Scripture names angels with bodily parts, animals, wheels, fire, metals, colors, and ranks. Celestial Hierarchy argues that such symbols can elevate readers when interpreted according to their purpose. Especially “unlike” images prevent the mistake of picturing incorporeal beings as literally splendid human-shaped objects; their very incongruity provokes inquiry into hidden powers. Symbolism is therefore not a disposable shell around pure concepts. Material forms meet embodied knowers at their level and can become vehicles of anagogical movement, lifting attention from sensible representation toward intelligible and divine meaning.',
    'This method also limits literalist angelology. The nine names arranged in triads are scriptural designations interpreted as orders of intelligent beings, but later charts often turn Dionysius’s fluid symbolic and participatory account into a rigid celestial bureaucracy. Names indicate characteristic activities and modes of receiving illumination, not independent species observed directly. The angels themselves mediate and interpret divine gifts. A responsible reading distinguishes the corpus’s argument from later artistic and scholastic elaborations while recognizing their debt to it. Hierarchy is a theory of sacred communication before it is a picture of ranked occupants of heaven.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'philosophy-of-religion', 'aesthetics'], [], ['Celestial Hierarchy I–XV']),
  section('hierarchy', 'Sacred order as purification, illumination, and perfection', [
    'Dionysius defines hierarchy through ordered participation in divine knowledge and activity. A hierarch receives illumination, becomes assimilated to God as far as possible, and communicates what can be received by those below. Purification, illumination, and perfection describe recurring functions rather than merely three bureaucratic offices. Celestial and ecclesiastical hierarchies mirror one another without becoming identical. Mediation protects difference of capacity and joins each participant to a community; no creature possesses the divine gift as private property. At its best, hierarchy names circulation: reception becomes generous transmission and return.',
    'The model also raises serious political and ethical questions. It sacralizes graded clerical roles, restricts some contemplative access, and describes excluded or subordinate groups through a late-antique ecclesial order. Modern readers should neither translate this directly into a timeless command for institutional inequality nor pretend that “hierarchy” means only a benign network. Dionysius presents differentiation as providential and cooperative, but later institutions could use his authority to naturalize power. Philosophical assessment therefore needs both internal purpose and reception: how mediation is supposed to prevent possessive domination, and how a sacred order can nevertheless authorize unequal voices and access.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-religion', 'ethics'], [], ['Celestial Hierarchy III', 'Ecclesiastical Hierarchy I']),
  section('liturgy', 'Bodies, rites, and contemplation in public', [
    'Ecclesiastical Hierarchy interprets visible rites as patterned disclosures of invisible realities. Baptism stages purification and new orientation; Eucharistic gathering distributes a unifying gift without erasing participants; consecrations and funerals locate lives within an ordered community. The material action is not merely a teaching aid to be discarded by advanced minds. Incense, oil, water, bread, movement, song, and spatial arrangement engage embodied persons and communicate through differentiated capacities. Dionysian contemplation is consequently public and liturgical as well as intellectual, a corrective to portraits of him as an advocate of solitary escape into featureless transcendence.',
    'Yet the text is an idealizing interpretation, not a neutral ethnography of all worship around 500. Scholars use its ritual details cautiously when locating the author, because related liturgical forms circulated across regions and editorial layering is possible. Its theological claim is clearer: symbols work through participation and authorized interpretation, joining bodily and intelligible dimensions. The relationship between this mediated ascent and Mystical Theology’s silence remains difficult. Rather than selecting one as authentic and rejecting the other, readers can see a productive tension between communal forms that disclose God and a transcendence no form, office, word, or intellect can contain.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'aesthetics', 'philosophy-of-mind'], [], ['Ecclesiastical Hierarchy II–VII', 'Mystical Theology']),
  section('evil', 'Evil as privation within providential order', [
    'Divine Names treats evil while explaining the Good. If all beings derive being and power from the Good, evil cannot be a self-subsisting principle or nature. Demons are not evil insofar as they exist; their powers are goods used defectively. Evil is lack, disorder, and failure relative to a nature’s proper activity. This account blocks metaphysical dualism and protects divine causality from producing evil as evil. It also creates a problem familiar from privation theories: suffering and wrongdoing appear forcefully active even if their agency borrows every capacity from goods they damage.',
    'Dionysius does not offer a modern account of structural evil, trauma, or political responsibility, and later readers should not use metaphysical privation to minimize experienced harm. Within his framework, calling evil nonbeing is not calling it harmless. Disorder corrupts participation, distorts desire, and requires purification and reorientation. Providence sustains the agent’s underlying good powers without endorsing their misuse. The analysis links ethics to metaphysics: return is possible because evil never becomes a creature’s deepest nature. But the corpus gives more attention to cosmic order and ritual restoration than to detailed psychology of culpability, leaving important questions for later interpreters.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['proclus'], ['Divine Names IV']),
  section('reception-reading', 'A corpus repeatedly translated into new authorities', [
    'The Dionysian writings rapidly acquired commentary and authority in Greek and Syriac settings, then traveled through Latin translations associated with Hilduin, John Scottus Eriugena, John Sarracenus, and Robert Grosseteste. Maximus the Confessor, Aquinas, Bonaventure, vernacular mystical authors, Nicholas of Cusa, Renaissance thinkers, and modern theologians received different Dionysii. Some emphasized angelic and ecclesial order, others divine naming, union, beauty, or negative theology. This range is evidence of generative ambiguity, not proof that every later doctrine was already present. Reception claims should identify a translator, commentary, or textual route rather than draw one unbroken line from “mysticism” to the present.',
    'Begin with Divine Names chapters 1–4 and Mystical Theology as a pair: map every affirmation to its causal basis and every negation to the creaturely limitation it removes. Then read Celestial Hierarchy chapters 1–3 on symbols and hierarchy before encountering the famous nine orders. Ecclesiastical Hierarchy chapters 1–3 reveals why bodies and rites cannot be omitted. A modern complete translation helps with technical terms; the SEP entry, Rorem, Louth, and the Oxford Handbook place authorship and reception under scrutiny. Always distinguish the anonymous author from Acts’ Dionysius, the late-antique corpus from medieval systems, and secure textual dependence from imagined personal biography.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'neoplatonism', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['proclus', 'aquinas'], ['Divine Names', 'Mystical Theology', 'Celestial Hierarchy', 'Ecclesiastical Hierarchy']),
];

