      intellectualDevelopment: ['Early anthropological and anti-Eunomian writings emerge from Basil’s legacy and immediate doctrinal controversy.', 'Later spiritual exegesis gives increasing prominence to divine infinity, darkness, and perpetual progress.', 'Differences among polemic, catechesis, dialogue, sermon, and spiritual commentary should not be forced into one timeless formula.'],
      influencesReceived: ['Christian scripture and pro-Nicene ecclesial argument', 'Basil of Caesarea, Macrina, and Gregory of Nazianzus', 'Origen and earlier Christian exegesis', 'Platonist, Aristotelian, Stoic, medical, and rhetorical traditions'], influenceOnLaterThought: ['Pseudo-Dionysius and Byzantine apophatic theology', 'Maximus the Confessor and Eastern Christian accounts of deification', 'Modern debates over embodiment, gender, slavery, and theological anthropology', 'Christian universalist and anti-universalist interpretations'],
      influencedByIds: ['origen', 'plato'], influencedIds: ['pseudo-dionysius'], disagreementIds: [],
      controversiesOrInterpretiveTensions: ['His biography and the chronology of individual works remain partly reconstructed.', 'Technical language develops in polemical contexts and should not be mapped mechanically onto later theology.', 'On the Making of Man permits sharply different evaluations of sex, gender, and embodiment.', 'The unity of universal human nature and individual persons is philosophically difficult.', 'Universal-restoration readings have strong textual evidence, but their scope, consistency, and relation to freedom remain disputed.', 'Later mystical and Palamite reception should not replace Gregory’s own fourth-century questions.'],
      commonMisunderstandings: ['Gregory is not merely a Christian translator of Plato.', 'Divine incomprehensibility does not make every theological statement false.', 'Spiritual ascent does not require contempt for bodies or resurrection.', 'Epektasis is not restless dissatisfaction but inexhaustible participation in goodness.', 'A few restoration passages do not eliminate every interpretive tension in his eschatology.', 'His ancient critique of slavery is philosophically remarkable without being identical to a modern abolitionist movement.'],
